Z||000001||onfire||10-06-2001||04:29 AM||||I am sorry I can't help you, but you just reminded me of a story.<P>My uncle used to own a 1969 Dodge Super Bee, he was building. He just got done with the 440 six-pac motor. Anyway he bet his friend he could light up the tires while doing about 45 m.p.h. His friend was like no way, not gonna happen. So my uncle mashed the throttle and lit them up like he was on ice. After about 25 feet he caught traction hard, and the driveshaft went through the floor. He replaced most of the floor with titanium sheet, he got from my dads work. The floor was rusting out anyway. Or atleast that's what he told his wife. He sold the car about a year later, to buy a house. <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/onfiresigtoo.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ray b||10-06-2001||01:05 PM||||SS will cut like steel hacksaw or cutoff wheel or grinder will work.<BR>the trick is to buff it after cut to remove iron from top layer by buffing and leave only the crome to contact the air.<BR>thats why a scrach will rust on SS.<BR>BTW rust is a bug that is alive and eats steel but crome kills the bugs/they can't eat it but a little scrach will let them get at the iron in the steel under the crome top layer.<BR>alllways buff cut SS or it will rust at the cuts.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
